  "answer_text": "Despite his name, Flounder is **not a real-life flounder**. \n\nInstead, he is a tropical reef fish. Because his design changed between the classic animation and the realistic CGI adaptation, his official species depends on which version of *The Little Mermaid* you are referring to:\n\n### 1. The 2023 Live-Action Remake: A **Sergeant Major Fish**\nIn the 2023 live-action adaptation, Flounder's design is based on a real-life fish called the **Sergeant Major** (*Abudefduf saxatilis*). This is a type of damselfish known for its yellow-and-silver body with dark, vertical stripes. \n\n### 2. The 1989 Animated Film: A stylized **Tropical Fish** (closely resembling a **Blue Angelfish**)\nIn the original 1989 animated movie, Flounder is highly stylized. He is a chubby, bright yellow fish with teal-blue stripes and round features. While Disney animators didn't officially name a species at the time, his cartoon look most closely resembles a **yellow/blue angelfish** or a similarly vibrant tropical reef fish. \n\n### Why is he named Flounder?\nHis name is an ironic joke. Real flounders are flat, brown bottom-dwellers with both of their eyes on one side of their head. They are masters of camouflage that blend into the muddy ocean floor\u2014the exact opposite of the bright, colorful, round little fish that follows Ariel around!",
